    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Irish and Scottish session dance types each carry a distinct pulse — matching foot-tap to meter is essential session etiquette."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "reels",
      "title": "Reels",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "2/2 or 4/4, steady driving beat. 'ONE-two THREE-four' at moderate-fast tempo. Most common session dance type."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "jigs",
      "title": "Jigs",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Double jig: 6/8, two beats per bar of three eighths — 'JIG-ged-y JIG-ged-y'. Single jig and slide: 12/8 variants regionally."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "waltzes-and-hornpipes",
      "title": "Waltzes and hornpipes",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Waltz: 3/4, strong beat one. Hornpipe: often dotted long-short rhythm, 4/4, slower bouncy feel — regional variation in dotting."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
